`build()` allocates the output via `String::new()` without a capacity hint, then `push_str` triggers geometric reallocations. The final length is known up-front (sum of non-empty content lengths in `parts`), so a single `String::with_capacity(total_len)` removes all intermediate reallocations. Minor on its own, but `build()` runs once per LLM request — and a cold-prompt warmup path is where these allocations matter.

```rust
let total: usize = sorted.iter()
    .filter(|(_, c)| !c.is_empty())
    .map(|(_, c)| c.len())
    .sum();
let mut out = String::with_capacity(total);
```

comments.suggestion_code:
pub fn build(self) -> String {
        let mut sorted = self.parts;
        // 显式按 slot priority 排序（priority 由 PromptSlot::priority 决定,不受枚举重排影响）
        sorted.sort_by_key(|(slot, _)| slot.priority());
        let total: usize = sorted
            .iter()
            .filter(|(_, c)| !c.is_empty())
            .map(|(_, c)| c.len())
            .sum();
        let mut out = String::with_capacity(total);
        for (_slot, content) in sorted {
            if !content.is_empty() {
                out.push_str(&content);
            }
        }
        out
    }

