comments.content:
No abort/cleanup for in-flight trace fetches on unmount or prop change. useEffect on [taskId, traceId] kicks off traceListByTask + traceDetail with no AbortController, no cancelled flag, and no finally to reset loading if the panel closes mid-fetch. If the user closes TracePanel while the request is still pending, the .then/.finally still call setState, which can produce React's "setState on unmounted component" warning and leave `loading=true` stuck if a re-mount happens before the late response arrives.

The detailSeqRef partially guards stale data overwrite but does NOT cover the traceListByTask() promise, the loading state, or the listWorkflowAudit() promise in loadAudit().

Minimal fix: use a `cancelled` flag (or AbortController) shared across reload() and loadAudit(), and bail out of all .then/.catch/.finally if set. Alternatively, since this is a portal-modal that mounts/unmounts the whole TracePanel, returning a cleanup from useEffect is sufficient for the mount-time fetch.

Example skeleton:
```ts
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;
  setLoading(true); setError(null);
  const run = async () => {
    try {
      if (traceId != null) {
        const d = await traceDetail(traceId);
        if (cancelled) return;
        ...
      } else {
        const list = await traceListByTask(taskId ?? "");
        if (cancelled) return;
        ...
      }
    } catch (e) { if (!cancelled) setError(String(e)); }
    finally { if (!cancelled) setLoading(false); }
  };
  run();
  return () => { cancelled = true; };
}, [taskId, traceId]);
```

Same treatment for loadAudit().

